# The Lambda MicroVM layer: the same crew image, started by the platform's hooks.
#
# Appended to the base recipe and the crew layer by
# `scripts/build_microvm_image_zip.py`, which is this lane's build TARGET -- the
# one thing that differs from Fargate. Fargate pushes the crew image to ECR and
# registers a task definition; this lane zips the same recipes and calls
# `CreateMicrovmImage`, and Lambda runs the build on ARM64 infrastructure. No
# image is pushed from an operator's machine on this lane and no registry
# credential exists on it.
#
# It COMPILES NOTHING OF OURS. Everything below is either a platform prerequisite
# the guest cannot work without, or the entrypoint. The serving code and the crew
# content are already in the image when this layer starts.

# Root for the layer, and root for PID 1. The hook listener has to write
# /etc/machine-id and register the SSM agent, neither of which a non-root process
# can do. The CREW does not run as root: the supervisor is spawned by the
# listener and the image's own `USER crew` posture is what the supervisor and its
# children inherit through the drop below.
USER root

# amazon-ssm-agent: the owner's ONLY route to this crew. A MicroVM's HTTPS
# endpoint reaches the hook port and nothing else, so the front process -- bound
# to loopback on this lane -- is reachable only through an SSM port-forward, and
# that needs the guest registered as a hybrid managed node.
#
# INSTALLED HERE, NEVER REGISTERED HERE. Every MicroVM from this image resumes
# from one snapshot, so a build-time registration would give every VM the same
# node identity and one private key: they would collide on a single `mi-` id and
# trip MachineFingerprintDoesNotMatch. Registration happens in the run hook, from
# the one-use activation the launch payload delivers.
#
# Staged with `dpkg -x` into a scratch directory and copied selectively, not
# `dpkg -i` and not `dpkg -x` into `/`. `dpkg -i` runs a postinst that enables a
# systemd unit, and there is no init system in this container -- the hook listener
# is the supervisor's parent. Unpacking into `/` replaces Debian's merged-usr
# `/lib` symlink with a real directory, which removes the dynamic loader from its
# expected path and breaks every dynamically linked binary in the image.
#
# sudo comes with it because `cloud/ssm.py` wraps every Run Command as
# `sudo -u <run_as> -i <cmd>` -- the same helper the EC2 and Fargate lanes use --
# so a guest without sudo answers every in-guest command with
# `sudo: command not found` and exit 127. One live MicroVM was spent learning
# that on 2026-10-06.
ARG SSM_REGION=us-east-1
RUN set -eux; \
    apt-get update; \
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends sudo; \
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*; \
    curl -fsSL -o /tmp/ssm.deb \
      "https://s3.${SSM_REGION}.amazonaws.com/amazon-ssm-${SSM_REGION}/latest/debian_arm64/amazon-ssm-agent.deb"; \
    dpkg -x /tmp/ssm.deb /tmp/ssmx; \
    cp -a /tmp/ssmx/usr/bin/. /usr/bin/; \
    mkdir -p /etc/amazon/ssm /var/lib/amazon/ssm /var/log/amazon/ssm; \
    cp -a /tmp/ssmx/etc/amazon/ssm/. /etc/amazon/ssm/; \
    cp /etc/amazon/ssm/seelog.xml.template /etc/amazon/ssm/seelog.xml; \
    rm -rf /tmp/ssm.deb /tmp/ssmx; \
    /usr/bin/amazon-ssm-agent -version; \
    test ! -e /var/lib/amazon/ssm/registration

# Two settings in this file are load-bearing, which is why it is a file:
#   Identity.ConsumptionOrder = ["OnPrem"] forces the hybrid-activation identity.
#     A MicroVM has no instance profile to remove, so this pin is the only guard
#     available against the agent looking for one.
#   Profile.ShareCreds = true makes the agent publish the node's role credentials
#     to root's shared credentials file. That file is the guest's ONLY AWS
#     identity -- there is no instance profile and nothing is baked in -- so
#     without it the guest can never read the secrets its payload references.
COPY microvm-ssm-agent.json /etc/amazon/ssm/amazon-ssm-agent.json

# Guest-only state, outside the data home so it cannot be swept into an archive.
# Created here, in the image, for the same reason the data directories are: a
# directory that does not exist is a boot that dies on a PermissionError reading
# like a code fault.
RUN mkdir -p /var/lib/microvm-guest /var/tmp/microvm-restore

# The crew user gets a real login shell on THIS lane, because SSM is how the
# owner reaches a MicroVM crew and `ssm:SendCommand` is wrapped in
# `sudo -u <user> -i bash`. The `-i` runs the account's login shell, and the base
# image creates `crew` with `--shell /usr/sbin/nologin` -- correct for a lane
# nobody shells into, and fatal here: every command exits non-zero before
# reaching what it was asked to run, with no output that names the shell as the
# cause.
#
# Scoped to this layer rather than changed in the base, because the Fargate lane
# reaches its crew through ECS exec and has no reason to widen the account.
RUN usermod -s /bin/bash crew

# A GATE on the crew layer's own file modes, not a fix for them: the fix is
# `COPY --chmod=0755` in Dockerfile.crew. This fails the build if the user the
# supervisor runs as cannot read the bundle it is about to install -- which is
# the failure one live MicroVM was spent diagnosing, and which presents as a
# corrupt-bundle error rather than a permission one.
#
# Here rather than in the crew layer because that file deliberately contains no
# RUN, and because a gate belongs with the layer that has root.
# It checks TRAVERSAL as well as readability, because the two fail separately: a
# file can be -rw-r--r-- inside a directory the reader cannot enter, which is
# what a mode without the execute bit produces -- `--chmod` applies one octal to
# every path the COPY creates, directories included, so `0644` there yields
# `drw-r--r--` and nothing under it can be reached.
#
# It asks whether every skill PRESENT is readable, not whether at least one
# exists. Demanding one made a crew with no skills unbuildable:
#
#     readable SKILL.md files: 0
#     no readable skill in the bundle
#
# and `packaging.build` advertises exactly that bundle ("Nothing private was
# selected: a valid bundle with the crew's persona only"), so the supported case
# failed at image build. Measured live. Counting present against readable keeps
# the whole point of the gate -- a skill the crew user cannot read is still a
# hard failure here rather than a corrupt-bundle error at run time -- while zero
# skills is zero of zero and passes.
RUN set -eux; \
    ls -ld /app /app/crew-bundle /app/crew-bundle/skills; \
    sudo -u crew test -x /app/crew-bundle \
      || { echo "crew cannot traverse /app/crew-bundle" >&2; exit 1; }; \
    for f in manifest.json agent.json mcp.json; do \
      sudo -u crew test -r "/app/crew-bundle/$f" \
        || { echo "crew cannot read /app/crew-bundle/$f" >&2; ls -l /app/crew-bundle >&2; exit 1; }; \
    done; \
    sudo -u crew test -x /app/crew-bundle/skills \
      || { echo "crew cannot traverse /app/crew-bundle/skills" >&2; exit 1; }; \
    present=$(sudo -u crew find /app/crew-bundle/skills -name 'SKILL.md' | wc -l); \
    readable=$(sudo -u crew find /app/crew-bundle/skills -name 'SKILL.md' -readable | wc -l); \
    echo "SKILL.md files: $present present, $readable readable"; \
    [ "$present" -eq "$readable" ] \
      || { echo "crew cannot read $((present - readable)) of $present skills" >&2; exit 1; }

# The front moves off the hook port. The platform's hook listener must own the
# port the image declares to `CreateMicrovmImage`, and the two cannot share one.
# Together with SMC_FRONT_BIND=127.0.0.1 in the run-hook environment this is what
# keeps the crew off the VM's public endpoint: a caller who mints an endpoint
# credential names a PORT, so the front must neither hold 8080 nor be bound to a
# routable address.
# Every crew route on this lane authenticates its caller, and that is set in the
# IMAGE rather than passed at launch.
#
# A MicroVM carries its own HTTPS endpoint, reachable from the internet, and the
# ingress connector does not govern it: the only control the platform enforces
# there is a port list, so a holder of a correctly-scoped token is an arbitrary
# internet caller. There is no security group to lean on, so the container
# authenticates for itself.
#
# Baked, not launched. A value in the run payload or in an image-level
# environment is one a caller can omit or replace, and the posture of a lane with
# no network bound must not depend on every launch remembering to ask for it.
# `ENV` here is part of the image: a VM started from it has the flag whatever the
# launch said.
#
# Fargate's default is unchanged -- that lane's flag is absent and so stays off,
# which is what its private subnet and zero-ingress security group already
# assert.
ENV SMC_HOOK_PORT=8080 \
    SMC_FRONT_PORT=8081 \
    SMC_FRONT_BIND=127.0.0.1 \
    SMC_REQUIRE_AUTH_ALL_ROUTES=1

EXPOSE 8080

# PID 1 is the hook listener, not the supervisor. Nothing else can be: the
# platform calls `/ready` during this very build, before any crew, credential or
# control secret exists, and the front refuses to start without all three.
#
# No HEALTHCHECK, for the reason the base gives: a check that proves the port is
# bound would report healthy on a crew whose every turn fails.
ENTRYPOINT ["python", "-m", "container.microvm.hooks"]
CMD []
